selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"#module-contents\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Module Contents<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-contents\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"#module-pyskyfire.thermo.eos\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.thermo.eos<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#module-pyskyfire.thermo.eos\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1><p>Equations of state: the thermodynamic relations a design reads a fluid through.</p><p>A turbomachine design needs more of a fluid than its density at one point. It\nneeds the states at either end of the machine, and the state an isentropic\nprocess reaches at the other end, since that fixes the head. A turbine\u2019s\nmeanline also needs the states between its rows, which it knows by enthalpy\nand by the entropy the losses so far have added, and the speed of sound at\neach. An <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"EquationOfState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.EquationOfState\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.EquationOfState\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">EquationOfState</span></code></a> answers those questions and returns the\npackage\u2019s <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../state/FluidState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidState</span></code></a> each time, so the code\nasking does not care whether the answer came from CoolProp or from a constant.</p>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.as_equation_of_state\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.as_equation_of_state\">\n<span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.eos.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">as_equation_of_state</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">fluid</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span> <span class=\"sig-return\"><span class=\"sig-return-icon\">\u2192</span> <span class=\"sig-return-typehint\"><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"EquationOfState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.EquationOfState\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.EquationOfState\"><span class=\"pre\">EquationOfState</span></a></span></span></dt><dd><p>Read <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">fluid</span></code> as an equation of state.</p><p>An <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"EquationOfState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.EquationOfState\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.EquationOfState\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">EquationOfState</span></code></a> is returned as it is. Anything else names a\nCoolProp fluid and is handed to <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"CoolPropEOS.html#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.CoolPropEOS\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.CoolPropEOS\"><code class=\"xref py py-class docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">CoolPropEOS</span></code></a>.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"CoolPropEOS.html#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.CoolPropEOS\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.CoolPropEOS\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.eos.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">CoolPropEOS</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">fluid</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">EquationOfState</span></code></p><p>A real fluid, from CoolProp.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>A <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(p,</span> <span class=\"pre\">T)</span></code> pair lying on the saturation line doesn\u2019t identify a state:\nit could be liquid, vapour or anything between. <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.CoolPropEOS.state_pT\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.CoolPropEOS.state_pT\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">state_pT()</span></code></a> reads\nsuch a pair as saturated liquid, a millikelvin into the liquid, because\na pump inlet drawn from a tank at its own vapour pressure is where this\ncomes up. Give the enthalpy through <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.CoolPropEOS.state_ph\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.CoolPropEOS.state_ph\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">state_ph()</span></code></a> to mean anything\nelse.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../state/FluidStateResolver.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidStateResolver\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidStateResolver\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.state.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidStateResolver</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">transport</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">p_ref</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Resolve fluid states from <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(h,</span> <span class=\"pre\">p)</span></code>, with cached saturation data.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"EquationOfState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.EquationOfState\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.EquationOfState\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.eos.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">EquationOfState</span></span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/abc.html#abc.ABC\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">abc.ABC</span></code></a></p><p>The state lookups a machine design asks of a fluid.</p><p>Every pressure is in Pa, temperature in K and specific enthalpy in\nJ kg\u207b\u00b9. Enthalpy is only ever compared with enthalpy from the same\nequation of state, so its datum does not matter.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../state/FluidState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.state.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidState</span></span></dt><dd><p>Resolved bulk fluid state at one axial station.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"Incompressible.html#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.Incompressible\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.Incompressible\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.eos.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">Incompressible</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">rho</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">cp</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">|</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/constants.html#None\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">EquationOfState</span></code></p><p>A fluid of constant density.</p><p>Enthalpy is <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">cp*T</span> <span class=\"pre\">+</span> <span class=\"pre\">p/rho</span></code>. With constant density, an isentropic\nprocess is also isothermal, so it moves only the <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">p/rho</span></code> term. The\nisentropic head between two pressures is then <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">\u0394p/rho</span></code> exactly: the\nfamiliar pump head.</p><p><code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">cp</span></code> is optional. Without it temperature is carried through isentropic\nprocesses but cannot be recovered from an enthalpy, so <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.Incompressible.state_ph\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.Incompressible.state_ph\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">state_ph()</span></code></a>\nreports it as NaN, and there is no entropy: <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.Incompressible.entropy\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.Incompressible.entropy\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">entropy()</span></code></a> and\n<a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#pyskyfire.thermo.eos.Incompressible.state_hs\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.eos.Incompressible.state_hs\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">state_hs()</span></code></a> need <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">cp</span></code>. Entropy is then <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">cp*ln(T)</span></code>, datum 1 K.\nSound is infinitely fast in an incompressible fluid.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#functions\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Functions<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#functions\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>", "a[href=\"#classes\"]": "<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Classes<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#classes\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h2>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(` ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 600, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
